<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; background-color: #e6f2f8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Pink Flowers! – Lesson # 4</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: 10px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Pink Flowers!</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple greetings on cards. Interactive: Write very simple sentences with assistance, even if errors are present.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple greetings or nature-related phrases on cards (e.g., "Happy garden day!"). Can write simple sentences about the garden or nature with teacher support (e.g., "This is a tall tree"). Can label pictures of plants or trees using simple words like "flowers," "trees," and "leaves".</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; background-color: #e6f2f8; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a large, colorful picture of a garden with many pink flowers. Teacher says, "Look! What do you see?" Students respond with words like "flower," "tree," "garden." Teacher asks, "What color are the flowers?" Elicits "pink." Teacher then holds up flashcards of "flower," "tree," "sun," and "leaf" and asks students to say the words. Teacher writes each word slowly on the board as students say them, connecting the spoken word to its written form. The teacher also shows a simple card with "Happy garden day!" written on it.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing the word "flower" on the board, sounding out each letter /f/ /l/ /o/ /w/ /e/ /r/ and emphasizing the initial /f/ sound. Then, the teacher models writing "pink" and "tree" (/t/ sound for 'tree'). Teacher demonstrates forming the letters clearly for students to see. For sentence modeling, the teacher writes "This is a flower" and "The flowers are pink" while pointing to corresponding items in the picture, verbalizing the process.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "flower" on the board) "Is this a tree? (No.) What is this?" (Flower!)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to "pink" written on the board) "What color is this word?" (Pink!)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, illustrated flashcards with nature scenes and simple labels. A large chart paper displaying simple sentences about a garden, such as "This is my garden. The flowers are pink. The tree is tall." Teacher also has blank "garden cards" for students to write on.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Linguistic Competence: Students learn to write simple noun + verb + adjective sentences and use present simple for descriptions of nature. Pragmatic Competence: Students use basic phrases to describe plants and ask questions about them. Sociolinguistic Competence: Students use respectful language when talking about nature.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher reads the sentences aloud, pointing to each word. "What is the text about? A garden or a house?" (A garden!) "What color are the flowers in the text?" (Pink!)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Teacher displays flashcards of a garden, pink flowers, and a tall tree. Students match the written sentence (e.g., "The flowers are pink.") to the correct picture.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing target vocabulary (garden, flower, tree, pink, tall) and simple sentences using "Noun + verb + adjective" (e.g., "The flowers are pink.") and "Present simple" (e.g., "This is a tall tree."). They will also practice writing the greeting "Happy garden day!".</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing and Matching (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with dotted outlines of target vocabulary words (flower, tree, garden, pink). Students trace the words. On the same sheet, there are simple pictures of these items, and students draw a line to match the traced word to the correct picture. The teacher circulates, providing individual help and correcting letter formation.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher provides worksheets with simple sentences about the garden with one word missing. Students choose from a word bank (e.g., pink, tall, flower, tree) to complete the sentence. Examples: "This is a _____." (picture of a flower, students write 'flower'). "The flowers are _____." (students write 'pink'). "The _____ is tall." (picture of a tree, students write 'tree'). Teacher emphasizes the /s/ sound in "sun" if it's in the word bank and used.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Sentence Writing (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives each pair a large picture of a simple garden scene with various elements (e.g., a tall tree, pink flowers, green leaves). On a separate piece of paper, students take turns writing one simple sentence about the picture using provided sentence starters: "This is a ____." "The ____ is ____." or "The ____ are ____." They check each other's sentences for legibility and correct word use (e.g., "This is a tall tree," "The flowers are pink"). Teacher assists by providing word spellings if needed.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual writing of a "Happy Garden Day" card with a simple drawing and labels.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher distributes blank paper folded in half (to resemble a card). On the front, students draw their own garden scene with at least one tree and some flowers (encouraging pink flowers to align with the theme). Inside the card, students write a greeting: "Happy garden day!" followed by one or two simple sentences describing their drawing. Possible sentences: "This is my garden." "The flowers are pink." "This is a tall tree." Teacher provides a word bank on the board with key vocabulary (garden, flower, tree, pink, tall) and the greeting "Happy garden day!" as a reference. Students are encouraged to label elements in their drawing (e.g., writing "flower" next to a flower, "tree" next to a tree). The focus is on applying the writing skills to create a meaningful product for the "It's Our Garden." scenario, expressing respect for nature.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and brief rubric for written output.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "Happy Garden Day" cards.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Legibility:</b> Is the writing clear and readable? (Yes/Needs practice)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Greeting:</b> Is "Happy garden day!" included? (Yes/No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least 2 target vocabulary words (e.g., flower, tree, pink, garden) used and correctly spelled? (Yes/Partial/No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Structure:</b> Are simple sentences (e.g., "This is a tall tree," "The flowers are pink") attempted, even with errors? (Yes/Partial/No)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up their cards. The teacher asks:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you write 'Happy garden day!'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you write about your garden or flowers?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was a little hard to write today?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The card-making activity worked well for production, but some students still struggled with letter formation for longer words like "garden." The sentence completion provided good scaffolding. I will provide more tracing opportunities for full sentences next time and prepare more individual word flashcards for students who need extra support with specific letter sounds like /g/ in "garden" and /t/ in "tree."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will use their created garden cards to describe their gardens to a partner and then present their favorite part of the garden to the class, integrating their writing with speaking and summarizing skills. They will also review all vocabulary from the unit.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>